A-1 (automated audit, zero violations), A-2 (both themes):
- `npx @axe-core/cli http://localhost:8094/whatif --tags
  wcag2a,wcag2aa,wcag21a,wcag21aa,wcag22aa --exit` -> "0 violations found!"
  (real server-rendered light-theme HTML, real stylesheet).
- Playwright + injected real axe-core 4.13.0 engine (axe.run with the same
  tag set) against the SAME server render, once with colorScheme:'light'
  and once with colorScheme:'dark' (the app's own `prefers-color-scheme`
  detection in base.html picks up the emulated scheme on cold load, no
  synthetic class injection) -> results.json: light axeViolations=[],
  dark axeViolations=[]. Screenshots: gv2-audit/whatif-light-full.png,
  gv2-audit/whatif-dark-full.png.
- Axe is necessary but not sufficient here: axe's color-contrast rule only
  checks DOM text, not Plotly SVG line/marker strokes, which is exactly
  where the failure below lives (found by the manual walk, not axe).

A-3 (contrast), manual walk, computed colors (verified independently with a
plain-Python WCAG contrast function, matches the in-page getComputedStyle
readings):
- Summary line (#projection-guardrail-summary, NEW in GV2): light
  color rgb(87,83,78) on bg rgb(255,255,255) = 7.63:1; dark color
  rgb(214,211,209) on bg rgb(41,37,36) = 10.18:1. Both pass 4.5:1 body text.
- Chart legend/axis text (existing getThemeColors() theming, applied to the
  new y2 axis too via axisKeys covering xaxis2/yaxis2): light
  rgb(68,64,60) on white; dark rgb(231,229,228) on rgb(41,37,36). Both pass.
- Chart line/marker colors carrying meaning (WCAG 1.4.11 / A-3 "icons/
  borders that carry meaning", >=3:1), measured against the card's real
  background (white in light, rgb(41,37,36) in dark; plot/paper bgcolor is
  transparent, confirmed in charts.js buildDefaultLayout, so the card bg is
  what's behind every stroke):
    "Cut trigger"   #ef4444 vs white = 3.76:1  PASS
    "Cut trigger"   #ef4444 vs dark  = 4.03:1  PASS
    "Raise trigger" #22c55e vs white = 2.28:1  FAIL (< 3:1)
    "Raise trigger" #22c55e vs dark  = 6.66:1  PASS
    "Guardrail cuts / raises" raise markers (triangle-up) reuse the same
      #22c55e: 2.28:1 white / 6.66:1 dark -> same FAIL in light only.
  Screenshots gv2-audit/whatif-light-card.png vs whatif-dark-card.png show
  it visually: the raise-trigger dashed line and the green triangle markers
  are visibly washed out against white in light mode and clearly legible
  in dark mode -- readable in one theme, not meeting AA contrast in the
  other (A-2's own framing).

Attribution (git diff master, per the checker's rule):
- The "Raise trigger" trace and its `"color": "#22c55e"` are new code added
  by this task (internal/handlers/whatif/handlers.go, the new
  `guardrailsEnabled` block appending "Cut trigger"/"Raise trigger" traces).
  This is an in-scope, newly-introduced contrast failure, not inherited.
- The raise-marker hex `#22c55e` itself is pre-existing (present at the same
  line in `git show master:internal/handlers/whatif/handlers.go`, used by
  the "Guardrail cuts / raises" trace before GV2). BUT GV2 is what moves
  that trace onto the new `yaxis: "y2"` panel
  (`if guardrailsEnabled { guardrailTrace["yaxis"] = "y2" }`, new in this
  diff) -- a panel with no area fill behind it, unlike the old placement
  which sat over the "Portfolio Balance" trace's `fill:"tozeroy"` light-green
  fill (a materially different, non-white background the color was
  presumably picked against). Per the checker's own rule ("replicated into
  new content" is in scope, not just literal new bytes), this is GV2's
  failure: it re-hosted a marginal pre-existing color choice onto a plain
  white/transparent panel where it now measurably fails 3:1. The
  "Portfolio Balance" line's own #22c55e stroke is untouched by this task
  and is OBSERVATION-only (see below).
- The "Cut trigger" counterpart uses #ef4444 and passes in both themes, so
  the asymmetry is specific to the green choice, not a systemic panel issue.

